A||||4||Voytek||Clutch Pedal Vibration - Any ideas?||||1||
Z||000000||Voytek||10-31-2001||01:12 PM||||Ok.  This is the story.  I've had my engine rebuilt in March.<BR>The reason: crank shaft bearing.<BR>The symptoms: loud clicking/metal-to-metal type noise from the engine when clutch pedal deppressed.<P>The shop said this: the crank bearing was toast and the crank (with the flywheel) was vibrating in the engine back and forth, hence the vibration and noise.<P>Current condition: no noise but vibration on clutch engagement.  I can particularly feel this when starting from a standstill uphill.  Other than that, always on engagement from standstill.  Sometimes I can feel it more and sometimes less.  The clutch pedal vibrates and you can feel it in the car a bit too.<P>QUESTION:  Before I go back to the shop and tear someone a new hole, I'd like to know if:<BR>#1.  Worn engine mounts could be causing this<BR>#2.  Something else could be causing this.<P>Thank you very much for any suggestions.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||10-31-2001||04:54 PM||||From your description the only thing I can think of that might "back feed" into the pedal is the clutch release bearing being worn out (should also be making a little noise).  As far as any body shake, engine and trans mounts should be looked at, as failures due occur which are most of the time overlooked because no big problem surfaces immediately.<P>Back to the clutch pedal, something has to be worn out to transfer that much feel back through the hydraulic system. You might as well check out the pedal bushing for wear at the same time.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Neal||10-31-2001||06:17 PM||||As to what california kid said the clutch system shouldnt transmit that much vibration.<P>When i broke my crankshft i couldnt feel anything in the clutch pedal, it felt totally normal.  So i think you probably have a different problem.<P>Motor mounts may be it but i would check the bellhousing bolts and the slave cylinder bolts, make sure they are tight.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||11-01-2001||10:42 PM||||It's most likely the springs on the pressure plate,uneven pressure from a tough installation,or abuse.If it were the bearing,you'd probably hear it somehow,and if the crank was moving that much,it wouldn't rev-up very smoothly at all.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Voytek||11-02-2001||11:03 AM||||Thanks a lot guys.  I will check all the mounts first.  I believe they're all original.  With 211,000 kms (about 132,000 mi) there is a chance one or two could be worn out.<P>If this doesn't help, back to the shop.  You see, it was the shop that rebuilt my engine that said the vibration was from the worn-out crank bearing.  After the rebuild, it seemed to have gone away for a bit (2 or 3 months) and now it's back.<P>lowCG - the clutch was new last year.  They've replaced ALL the components (supposedly).  It also seemed to help for a bit (then the noise and vibration started and I had the engine rebuilt).<P>Either way, the engine may have to come out if it's not the mounts.<P>Any other ideas?||||reg||1
